WEBVTT

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:13.860
<b>Everything Becomes F: The Perfect Insider</b>

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:14.070
<b>Everything Becomes F: The Perfect Insider</b>

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:37.030
<b>Everything Becomes F: The Perfect Insider</b>

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:42.910
<b>Chapter Nine                  Yellow Blind Spot</b>

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:10.150
This ship's totally different than when we arrived.

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:13.580
U-Um...

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:18.160
How far is the walk to Magata Labs?

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:24.050
Thirty minutes, but there was an incident.

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:25.040
An incident?

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:26.710
Is this okay?

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:27.840
Ask me later.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:39.130
Excuse me.

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:43.400
I'm Shibaike, with the Aichi Prefectural Police.

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:46.850
You're Saikawa-sensei and Nishinosono-san, yes?

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:47.810
Yes.

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:50.740
This is Director Shindo's wife.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:52.240
Hello.

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:57.210
I've heard the basic details. 
Could you show me around?

00:02:58.760 --> 00:02:59.820
Nishinosono-san?

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:03.780
Please try not to wander around.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:05.230
I refuse.

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:09.400
The chief asked me to ensure that 
you were absolutely safe.

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:11.160
Please cooperate.

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:14.500
Uncle...

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:18.170
That's impossible, Moe-chan.

00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:19.630
Uncle!

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:23.540
A day or two is one thing, given the location,

00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:27.350
but I can't cover up someone's death for a week.

00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:31.870
Please, Uncle... I promised the people here!

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:34.810
Fine. Here's what we'll do.

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:38.070
I'll make time to visit there this evening.

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:41.520
I'll speak to the laboratory staff directly.

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:44.950
You tell the police everything that happened.

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:46.800
No need to worry about anything.

00:03:47.770 --> 00:03:49.090
All right.

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:52.710
Saikawa-sensei...

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:55.930
That's enough. Let's tell them everything.

00:03:56.620 --> 00:04:00.000
I feel bad for Yamane-san, but I've made my choice.

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:02.600
I'm going to tell them everything.

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:04.790
I'm glad.

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:21.610
I'm sorry I couldn't keep my promise.

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:27.250
No, I'm the one who made
 an unreasonable request.

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:30.580
Now that this has happened,

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:33.620
I absolutely want to know who did this.

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:37.240
<i>Saikawa-sensei, Detective Shibaike is calling you.</i>

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:39.260
Oh, Deborah?

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:43.130
Resetting Red Magic brought her back on-line.

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:45.720
I'll be right back.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:47.600
Yamane-san, excuse me.

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:48.440
Sensei...

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:51.170
Sorry, I'll be going, too.

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:55.700
Okay!

00:04:58.110 --> 00:04:59.520
Come in.

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:00.840
Nishinosono Moe.

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:07.270
Excuse me.

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:08.030
Oh!

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:10.280
Nishinosono-san?!

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:13.270
Gido-san, right?

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:15.290
So you work here?

00:05:15.290 --> 00:05:18.750
No, I just arrived.

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:21.250
What about you? What are you doing here?

00:05:21.550 --> 00:05:23.090
Well, you know...

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:24.550
You know her?

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:27.540
You're here for a ladies' magazine interview?

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:30.090
That's right... <i>Miss Trend</i>.

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:31.360
You know it, right?

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:32.470
No, never heard of it.

00:05:32.470 --> 00:05:36.080
Oh, it isn't well known here.

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:40.360
They're taking interviews and photos, 
for a two-page spread!

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:41.320
I don't know what to say.

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:44.940
So, what's happening with the case?

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:46.450
You told her?

00:05:46.450 --> 00:05:50.450
With all these cops around, it would 
be more suspicious if I kept quiet.

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:54.450
Hey, who killed Dr. Magata Shiki?

00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:55.870
Who are the suspects?

00:05:55.870 --> 00:05:58.640
There can't be too many somewhere like this.

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:00.900
It's not that simple.

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:03.570
A week ago, she was alive.

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:07.220
And since then, no one's entered or left her room.

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:09.080
But she was killed.

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:11.350
It's been over a week.

00:06:11.350 --> 00:06:13.560
No one's ever been in there.

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:15.400
Or so you think, but actually—

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:16.910
Actually nothing!

00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:18.640
We investigated ourselves!

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:20.020
We?

00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:21.130
Who is we?

00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:22.730
Saikawa-sensei and I.

00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:25.910
Sohei-kun's here?

00:06:25.910 --> 00:06:27.610
What? Why?

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:30.580
What is he doing in a place like this?

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:32.170
He came with me.

00:06:32.170 --> 00:06:33.380
For work?

00:06:33.380 --> 00:06:34.790
The two of us came together.

00:06:34.790 --> 00:06:37.210
Then work had nothing to do with it?

00:06:37.210 --> 00:06:40.040
He loves computers,

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:43.800
but he spends most of his time wandering 
around, looking at old buildings, right?

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:47.920
He keeps photographing buildings that 
look on the verge of collapse.

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:51.950
How did you get to the island, Gido-san?

00:06:51.950 --> 00:06:53.010
A raft?

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:54.460
A raft?

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:56.290
Or did you swim?

00:06:56.290 --> 00:06:57.580
What?

00:06:57.580 --> 00:06:59.050
I took a ship. A plain old ship.

00:06:59.050 --> 00:07:00.270
You're boring.

00:07:01.010 --> 00:07:02.470
Come to think of it,

00:07:02.470 --> 00:07:07.900
when I arrived, there were 
several people leaving...

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:11.570
Were those Sohei-kun's students?

00:07:11.570 --> 00:07:12.780
You're mistaken.

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:14.710
No, they must have been!

00:07:14.710 --> 00:07:17.400
He said he was going camping.

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:18.830
You misheard him.

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:20.940
What?

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:23.430
You two coming here together was a lie?

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:26.460
The two of us and nine extras!

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:28.590
Extras?

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:30.990
There were nine or so, sure.

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:35.070
Two women... And then, a bunch of young men.

00:07:35.570 --> 00:07:37.100
Where do you live?

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:38.820
Yokohama.

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:42.200
Sohei-kun goes to Tokyo a lot, doesn't he?

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:44.980
He uses my place like a hotel!

00:07:44.980 --> 00:07:47.760
But I get some change out of it,

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:49.810
so I can't complain.

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:52.670
What's wrong? Do you not feel well?

00:07:56.080 --> 00:07:59.570
Everyone, give me some space with that.

00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:07.740
Saikawa-sensei stays in a girl's room.

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:09.970
In her room, of all people...

00:08:09.970 --> 00:08:11.940
He uses it as a hotel? That's wrong!

00:08:12.260 --> 00:08:13.430
Change!

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:16.430
Of course he wouldn't do that!

00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:18.930
What?

00:08:24.290 --> 00:08:25.740
I finally found you.

00:08:26.150 --> 00:08:27.340
You're late.

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:29.790
I asked a lot of people where you were.

00:08:29.790 --> 00:08:30.850
You're lying.

00:08:30.850 --> 00:08:34.500
If that were true, you should 
have been here sooner.

00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:36.950
What were you doing?

00:08:36.950 --> 00:08:40.730
I was looking into how those 
security videos are recorded.

00:08:43.510 --> 00:08:45.270
What are you doing?

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:46.980
I told you to give me space.

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:48.240
But...

00:08:48.610 --> 00:08:51.960
The videos of Dr. Magata's door

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:54.970
are recorded and compressed once a minute.

00:08:55.360 --> 00:08:57.670
I'm writing a script to check

00:08:57.670 --> 00:09:01.570
if these files match the times 
that they were recorded.

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:05.940
I get it.

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:09.750
You were looking for any gaps in the files.

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:13.070
You're sharper than the detective.

00:09:13.650 --> 00:09:15.560
If there's a missing file,

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:18.450
it would mean that footage is missing.

00:09:18.910 --> 00:09:21.740
And it might have shown the killer exiting the room.

00:09:21.740 --> 00:09:23.000
Correct.

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:24.500
And?

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:26.840
Nothing was missing.

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:28.800
The files were all there.

00:09:29.430 --> 00:09:30.680
Oh.

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:34.810
I looked for copies and dummies, 
but there wasn't anything.

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:41.180
In other words, it's more proof that 
nothing was done to that footage.

00:09:41.510 --> 00:09:45.520
But is the footage really that important?

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:46.640
Of course.

00:09:47.170 --> 00:09:51.460
It's the one piece of physical evidence 
that turns her living quarters

00:09:51.460 --> 00:09:52.680
into a perfect locked room.

00:09:53.170 --> 00:09:54.030
And...

00:09:54.030 --> 00:09:55.030
And?

00:09:55.640 --> 00:09:57.190
My hypothesis requires

00:09:57.490 --> 00:10:00.590
a way to get through that door, no matter what.

00:10:01.330 --> 00:10:02.830
Hypothesis?

00:10:02.830 --> 00:10:06.070
Do you know who the killer is?

00:10:06.670 --> 00:10:10.630
If nothing else, I know how the killer got in.

00:10:10.630 --> 00:10:12.510
You didn't tell me that!

00:10:12.510 --> 00:10:15.370
And what do you mean that

00:10:15.370 --> 00:10:16.940
I didn't see Dr. Magata!

00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:18.340
Tell me, please!

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:23.890
I'll figure it out for myself.

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:25.960
I know you can do it.

00:10:28.700 --> 00:10:29.900
Fifteen years ago,

00:10:30.310 --> 00:10:33.070
she went into that room herself.

00:10:33.370 --> 00:10:36.900
But at some point, the killer entered, too.

00:10:38.630 --> 00:10:40.040
Oh, jeez!

00:10:40.040 --> 00:10:42.030
Fifteen years ago, she was alone.

00:10:44.350 --> 00:10:46.330
But in that room,

00:10:47.340 --> 00:10:48.290
there were two.

00:10:50.770 --> 00:10:53.380
Magata Shiki and the killer?

00:10:57.330 --> 00:10:58.450
Sensei...

00:11:02.300 --> 00:11:03.590
I, too, have

00:11:04.490 --> 00:11:05.520
figured it out.

00:11:20.950 --> 00:11:23.950
We thought, except for Dr. Magata, no one

00:11:24.330 --> 00:11:27.120
went in or out of that door for fifteen years.

00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:31.930
But the killer went in, as well.

00:11:32.610 --> 00:11:34.860
They went into the room together.

00:11:46.520 --> 00:11:49.480
There was a robot named Michiru in the room.

00:11:49.840 --> 00:11:53.040
Michiru could lock and unlock the door.

00:11:53.980 --> 00:11:58.820
It existed because of someone who 
was always locking the door.

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:01.170
And of course, that person

00:12:02.150 --> 00:12:03.490
was not Dr. Magata.

00:12:06.150 --> 00:12:09.240
Even when I lost my parents, I had you.

00:12:09.730 --> 00:12:11.740
That's why I've survived this long.

00:12:12.580 --> 00:12:16.340
Like me, she lost her parents,

00:12:16.340 --> 00:12:18.370
and she had the same kind of person.

00:12:19.580 --> 00:12:20.550
Right here.

00:12:21.770 --> 00:12:23.210
Sachiro-san.

00:12:23.210 --> 00:12:26.550
As he fell to the ground, he screamed, 
"I won't let you do this!"

00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:30.340
That's what he meant

00:12:30.340 --> 00:12:31.970
as he collapsed.

00:12:32.630 --> 00:12:35.450
He couldn't allow his young daughter's pregnancy.

00:12:36.010 --> 00:12:38.370
Fifteen years ago, when she entered that room,

00:12:38.370 --> 00:12:40.210
she was alone.

00:12:40.840 --> 00:12:43.900
But one day, there were two people.

00:12:44.500 --> 00:12:45.780
That second person

00:12:46.350 --> 00:12:49.870
could have only been the child she gave birth to.

00:12:51.810 --> 00:12:56.540
That's how the killer got inside 
that perfect locked room.

00:12:58.780 --> 00:13:01.670
Her child was the one who killed her.

00:13:02.830 --> 00:13:04.680
The child killed its own mother,

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:07.600
amputated her limbs, used her fingerprints to leave,

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:10.460
had Michiru lock the door from 
the inside, and escaped!

00:13:12.680 --> 00:13:14.060
Why?

00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:16.190
You're thinking the same thing, aren't you?

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:18.350
That isn't enough.

00:13:18.880 --> 00:13:22.840
Even if you know how the killer got in, 
you don't know how they got out.

00:13:23.290 --> 00:13:24.950
Until you can prove that,

00:13:25.480 --> 00:13:27.860
you can't treat anyone as the criminal.

00:13:27.860 --> 00:13:28.740
But...

00:13:28.740 --> 00:13:29.660
Her daughter.

00:13:30.590 --> 00:13:32.190
It was a girl.

00:13:32.190 --> 00:13:35.460
Dr. Magata gave birth to a girl in that room.

00:13:36.310 --> 00:13:38.460
How do you know?

00:13:41.530 --> 00:13:42.460
Sensei,

00:13:43.330 --> 00:13:46.970
you can see something I can't, right?

00:13:47.860 --> 00:13:49.220
I won't deny that.

00:13:49.870 --> 00:13:55.700
Then, do you want to see what she saw?

00:13:55.700 --> 00:13:56.840
I do.

00:13:58.830 --> 00:14:00.230
That's why

00:14:00.680 --> 00:14:02.010
I'm here with you.

00:14:10.240 --> 00:14:12.240
Sensei, what time is it?

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:14.970
Um... 6:22.

00:14:14.970 --> 00:14:16.500
I've made up my mind!

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:18.620
I'll do it in the next thirty-eight minutes.

00:14:18.870 --> 00:14:21.310
I'll solve the case by 7:00.

00:14:21.630 --> 00:14:22.560
Let's do it!

00:14:22.560 --> 00:14:24.800
O-Okay... But we can't.

00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:28.030
Why not? There's no reason we can't!

00:14:28.030 --> 00:14:30.500
I haven't synchronized it in two days.

00:14:30.500 --> 00:14:31.800
It's a little off.

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:33.860
That doesn't matter!

00:14:33.860 --> 00:14:36.000
Within thirty-eight minutes, more or less!

00:14:37.640 --> 00:14:40.480
Sensei, why are you freezing?

00:14:40.480 --> 00:14:42.060
Why...

00:14:42.060 --> 00:14:43.810
Why am I so stupid?!

00:14:43.810 --> 00:14:44.890
Sensei?

00:14:44.890 --> 00:14:46.540
Why didn't I figure this out until now?

00:14:46.540 --> 00:14:47.900
Wait, Sensei!

00:14:49.230 --> 00:14:50.260
Hasebe-san!

00:14:51.310 --> 00:14:55.650
This room is isolated from Red Magic, right?

00:14:56.250 --> 00:14:57.620
Y-Yes...

00:14:57.620 --> 00:14:59.250
What about the computer's clock?

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:02.790
The clock alone is connected by a special cable.

00:15:03.090 --> 00:15:07.010
The clocks on all the lab's computers are synced.

00:15:07.010 --> 00:15:10.500
Call up the script I just wrote.

00:15:10.770 --> 00:15:14.210
Run it on today's data.

00:15:16.170 --> 00:15:17.640
I don't believe it!

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:19.270
What's going on?

00:15:19.270 --> 00:15:23.770
The file between 12:00 and 12:01 is missing!

00:15:24.020 --> 00:15:25.750
What is this?

00:15:25.750 --> 00:15:28.030
This is the logical conclusion.

00:15:28.030 --> 00:15:29.530
Yes. And?

00:15:29.530 --> 00:15:32.760
Nishinosono-kun, call me an idiot.

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:34.030
Idiot.

00:15:34.030 --> 00:15:36.110
It isn't enough! It isn't insulting enough!

00:15:36.110 --> 00:15:38.060
I want to insult myself!

00:15:39.470 --> 00:15:41.880
Did something happen today, at 12:00?

00:15:41.880 --> 00:15:43.960
What happened at 11:00 today?

00:15:44.280 --> 00:15:46.050
11:00?

00:15:48.630 --> 00:15:49.640
The power outage...

00:15:50.040 --> 00:15:53.600
But that was because Yamane-san reset Red Magic.

00:15:53.950 --> 00:15:56.900
Yes, and that set the time properly.

00:15:57.640 --> 00:16:00.630
From the start, the time was off in the entire lab.

00:16:01.080 --> 00:16:04.100
It was one minute behind the real time.

00:16:04.350 --> 00:16:07.520
But Red Magic's reset meant that the proper time

00:16:07.520 --> 00:16:10.080
came in over the network.

00:16:10.080 --> 00:16:12.780
The delayed minute needed to be adjusted for.

00:16:13.080 --> 00:16:16.380
So because the system adjusted its time,

00:16:16.380 --> 00:16:20.000
there is no file for 12:00.

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:21.410
Wait.

00:16:21.410 --> 00:16:23.640
The time in the lab was wrong?

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:25.810
The killer adjusted the clocks.

00:16:26.150 --> 00:16:28.170
They were deliberately delayed by a minute.

00:16:29.140 --> 00:16:30.950
What happens if you change the time?

00:16:31.410 --> 00:16:35.510
And how would that file be recorded, 
after the time's set back one minute?

00:16:36.190 --> 00:16:41.270
There would be an overlap, and the two 
files would have the same name.

00:16:41.650 --> 00:16:43.420
Would they both be saved?

00:16:43.420 --> 00:16:44.190
No.

00:16:44.190 --> 00:16:45.940
They'd have the same name,

00:16:45.940 --> 00:16:49.020
so the new file would overwrite the old.

00:16:49.020 --> 00:16:51.210
That's how computers work.

00:16:51.510 --> 00:16:53.810
Which means the old file...

00:16:53.810 --> 00:16:55.070
It would disappear.

00:16:55.260 --> 00:16:56.770
If you messed with the time,

00:16:56.770 --> 00:16:59.800
you could make that one minute 
disappear, couldn't you?

00:17:00.310 --> 00:17:03.060
We thought we had all the data,

00:17:03.060 --> 00:17:06.230
but there was actually one 
missing minute of footage!

00:17:06.530 --> 00:17:07.790
That's right.

00:17:08.030 --> 00:17:12.290
During that one minute, the killer 
passed through the yellow door.

00:17:12.730 --> 00:17:15.800
That's the trick they used to exit, isn't it?

00:17:15.800 --> 00:17:16.640
Yes.

00:17:16.930 --> 00:17:19.810
And they took the elevator to the roof.

00:17:19.810 --> 00:17:22.390
They didn't call it from the roof.

00:17:22.390 --> 00:17:24.670
They went from the basement to the roof.

00:17:25.060 --> 00:17:28.060
There, they waited for Director Shindo,
 and murdered him.

00:17:29.690 --> 00:17:31.070
Yay!

00:17:31.450 --> 00:17:33.070
You're a lot of fun!

00:17:33.070 --> 00:17:35.810
I'd never want to be your friend, though.

00:17:36.260 --> 00:17:38.180
What a coincidence! I agree!

00:17:42.030 --> 00:17:43.460
Sohei-kun!

00:17:43.460 --> 00:17:46.500
Why so pale?

00:17:46.500 --> 00:17:50.200
Setsuko, I'd love to tell you not to talk that way,

00:17:50.500 --> 00:17:51.810
but it doesn't matter right now.

00:17:52.110 --> 00:17:52.840
Shimada-san?

00:17:53.860 --> 00:17:56.480
You were looking at the Red Magic source, right?

00:17:56.840 --> 00:17:58.670
Can I see a list of something?

00:18:01.600 --> 00:18:04.490
Search for variables that call the time.

00:18:04.490 --> 00:18:08.040
There are a lot of them. Where am I looking?

00:18:08.040 --> 00:18:10.590
Red Magic's entire main system.

00:18:15.540 --> 00:18:16.290
No.

00:18:18.980 --> 00:18:20.900
No, that isn't it.

00:18:22.870 --> 00:18:24.200
No. No.

00:18:24.720 --> 00:18:26.460
It should be an increment equals.

00:18:26.780 --> 00:18:28.960
What variable type? There are too many.

00:18:29.410 --> 00:18:30.840
Most likely, an integer.

00:18:31.700 --> 00:18:32.750
What's he talking about?

00:18:32.750 --> 00:18:35.440
An integer. A whole number, probably.

00:18:35.870 --> 00:18:37.000
What about it?

00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:38.390
Please shut up.

00:18:38.390 --> 00:18:39.220
No.

00:18:39.550 --> 00:18:40.790
That isn't it, either.

00:18:40.790 --> 00:18:44.260
Come to think of it, there was 
one weird if statement

00:18:44.260 --> 00:18:46.730
I didn't understand in the mail system.

00:18:46.730 --> 00:18:47.870
Bring it up.

00:18:48.810 --> 00:18:51.560
Yes, search for this variable.

00:18:54.460 --> 00:18:55.930
I can't believe it...

00:18:56.230 --> 00:18:59.490
This is the part that opens Dr. Magata's door.

00:18:59.490 --> 00:19:01.550
I can't believe it was hard-coded.

00:19:01.870 --> 00:19:04.920
This is the hourglass I was searching for.

00:19:05.240 --> 00:19:06.870
Hourglass?

00:19:06.870 --> 00:19:09.310
What is the system call doing?

00:19:09.610 --> 00:19:10.710
Getting the time.

00:19:10.710 --> 00:19:12.330
And the variable definition?

00:19:12.800 --> 00:19:15.250
A global static integer.

00:19:15.250 --> 00:19:16.850
Unsigned short.

00:19:17.200 --> 00:19:18.320
What's this?

00:19:18.820 --> 00:19:20.800
Integers are two bytes, right?

00:19:20.800 --> 00:19:22.060
They are.

00:19:22.060 --> 00:19:23.250
Nishinosono-kun...

00:19:23.250 --> 00:19:24.390
Y-Yes?

00:19:24.620 --> 00:19:27.270
Multiply 256 by 256.

00:19:27.790 --> 00:19:30.140
65,536.

00:19:30.410 --> 00:19:34.770
How long ago was 65,535 hours 
before 7:00 AM two days ago?

00:19:35.020 --> 00:19:37.850
Subtract 19. Divide by 24.

00:19:37.850 --> 00:19:40.700
Take the remainder. It's August.

00:19:40.700 --> 00:19:42.030
And leap years...

00:19:42.690 --> 00:19:46.690
February 10, 4:00 AM, seven years ago.

00:19:47.530 --> 00:19:48.540
Isn't that when

00:19:48.860 --> 00:19:51.940
Red Magic, version 4, came on-line?

00:19:53.060 --> 00:19:54.040
Um...

00:19:54.040 --> 00:19:56.720
Yes... That's right.

00:19:57.040 --> 00:19:59.540
That explains why Red Magic went out of control,

00:19:59.540 --> 00:20:05.430
and why she hid versions she'd
 completed a long time ago.

00:20:06.700 --> 00:20:08.310
You're amazing, Nishinosono-kun.

00:20:08.730 --> 00:20:11.740
That calculation was something 
only you could see.

00:20:12.540 --> 00:20:13.910
I want to show my respect.

00:20:14.450 --> 00:20:15.300
Thank you.

00:20:17.170 --> 00:20:18.300
Nishinosono-kun...

00:20:18.300 --> 00:20:19.070
Yes?

00:20:19.070 --> 00:20:20.690
It's almost 7.

00:20:21.340 --> 00:20:22.150
Yes.

00:20:29.220 --> 00:20:30.190
Moe-chan...

00:20:31.050 --> 00:20:32.160
Uncle!

00:20:33.720 --> 00:20:36.590
I'm relieved to see your face!

00:20:37.460 --> 00:20:40.020
It's been a long time, Saikawa-sensei.

00:20:40.020 --> 00:20:40.760
Yes.

00:20:41.060 --> 00:20:43.960
So, you had something you wanted to discuss?

00:20:43.960 --> 00:20:48.810
Allow me to explain how this
 locked-room murder took place.

00:20:49.190 --> 00:20:50.350
Sensei,

00:20:50.350 --> 00:20:52.810
we haven't found a way in or out yet.

00:20:53.110 --> 00:20:56.020
I'll explain it in front of the killer right now.

00:20:56.560 --> 00:21:00.690
If I tell them I know the truth, 
they'll confess everything.

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:03.360
How can you say that?

00:21:04.020 --> 00:21:05.240
Their pride.

00:21:05.640 --> 00:21:07.870
Their pride as a human being.

00:21:08.180 --> 00:21:10.580
You know who the killer is, then?

00:21:10.890 --> 00:21:11.600
Yes.

00:21:15.390 --> 00:21:17.280
We finally meet,

00:21:17.810 --> 00:21:19.270
Saikawa-sensei.

00:21:19.750 --> 00:21:20.590
Hello,

00:21:21.590 --> 00:21:23.100
Dr. Magata Shiki.
